\section{Introduction}\label{sec:deductive-intro}

Deductive verification is the practice of proving that a program satisfies its
specification using logical reasoning.  Unlike testing, which can reveal the
presence of bugs but never their absence, and unlike model checking, which is
limited to finite-state systems, deductive verification can establish the
\emph{total correctness} of programs operating over infinite domains---provided
the verifier is supplied with sufficient annotations (invariants, preconditions,
postconditions).

The intellectual foundations of deductive verification lie in Hoare
logic~\cite{hoare1969axiomatic}, which provides a proof system for reasoning
about imperative programs.  Modern verification systems such as
Dafny~\cite{leino2010dafny}, F*~\cite{swamy2016fstar}, Why3~\cite{filliatre2013why3},
and Vercors~\cite{vercors2020} build on decades of research in program logic,
automated theorem proving, and type theory to bring deductive verification
closer to practical use.

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of deductive verification.
We begin with Hoare logic (\cref{sec:deductive-hoare}), proceed to verification
condition generation (\cref{sec:deductive-vcg}), examine invariant generation
(\cref{sec:deductive-invariants}), then study separation logic for heap programs
(\cref{sec:deductive-separation}).  We discuss modular verification
(\cref{sec:deductive-modular}) and survey modern tools
(\cref{sec:deductive-tools}).

