\section{Verification Condition Generation}\label{sec:deductive-vcg}

\subsection{From Proofs to Conditions}\label{sec:vcg-overview}

\emph{Verification condition generation} (VCG) is the process of reducing a
Hoare logic proof to a set of logical formulae (the \emph{verification
conditions}) that must be proved to establish the program's correctness.  The
key insight is that once the user has supplied loop invariants, the remaining
proof obligations can be generated automatically.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Verification Condition Generation}\label{alg:vcg}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Annotated command $c$ with invariants, precondition $P$, postcondition $Q$
\Ensure Set of verification conditions (logical formulae)
\Function{VC}{$c, Q$}
  \Switch{$c$}
    \Case{$x := e$}
      \State \Return $\{Q[e/x]\}$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$c_1; c_2$}
      \State $V_2 \gets \Call{VC}{c_2, Q}$
      \State $R \gets \mathit{wp}(c_2, Q)$ \Comment{or strongest postcondition}
      \State $V_1 \gets \Call{VC}{c_1, R}$
      \State \Return $V_1 \cup V_2$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ c_1\ \mathbf{else}\ c_2$}
      \State $V_1 \gets \Call{VC}{c_1, Q}$
      \State $V_2 \gets \Call{VC}{c_2, Q}$
      \State \Return $V_1 \cup V_2$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{inv}\ I\ \mathbf{do}\ c$}
      \State $V_{\mathit{body}} \gets \Call{VC}{c, I}$
      \State \Return $V_{\mathit{body}} \cup \{P \implies I, I \land \neg b \implies Q\}$
    \EndCase
  \EndSwitch
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Strongest Postconditions}\label{sec:vcg-sp}

An alternative to weakest preconditions is to compute \emph{strongest
postconditions} forward through the program.

\begin{definition}[Strongest Postcondition]\label{def:sp}
The \emph{strongest postcondition} $\mathit{sp}(P, c)$ of a command $c$ with
respect to a precondition $P$ is the strongest predicate $Q$ such that
$\{P\}\; c\; \{Q\}$ holds.
\end{definition}

The forward approach is sometimes more natural for verification condition
generation, as it follows the flow of the program:
\begin{align*}
  \mathit{sp}(P, x := e) &= \exists x_0.\; P[x_0/x] \land x = e[x_0/x] \\
  \mathit{sp}(P, c_1; c_2) &= \mathit{sp}(\mathit{sp}(P, c_1), c_2) \\
  \mathit{sp}(P, \mathbf{assert}(\phi)) &= P \land \phi
\end{align*}

\subsection{The Role of SMT Solvers}\label{sec:vcg-smt}

Modern verification tools discharge verification conditions using SMT solvers.
The typical workflow is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The user annotates the program with specifications and invariants.
\item The VCG tool generates verification conditions (VCs).
\item The VCs are translated to SMT-LIB format and passed to a solver.
\item If all VCs are proved, the program is verified.
\item If any VC fails, the tool reports a potential error with a counterexample.
\end{enumerate}

