\section{Invariant Generation}\label{sec:deductive-invariants}

\subsection{The Challenge}\label{sec:invariant-challenge}

Loop invariants are the Achilles' heel of deductive verification.  While
specifications (pre/post-conditions) express \emph{what} the program should do,
invariants express \emph{why} it does it---the inductive hypothesis that makes
the correctness argument go through.  Finding invariants automatically is a
deep problem.

\subsection{Abstract Interpretation for Invariants}\label{sec:invariant-ai}

Abstract interpretation (\cref{ch:ai-theory}) can compute loop invariants
automatically by computing a fixed point of the abstract semantics.

\begin{example}[Interval Invariant]\label{ex:interval-inv}
Consider the loop:
\begin{verbatim}
  i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    a[i] = 0;
    i = i + 1;
  }
\end{verbatim}
Using interval abstract interpretation, we can automatically discover the
invariant $0 \leq i \leq n$, which is sufficient to prove that the array access
$a[i]$ is in bounds.
\end{example}

\subsection{Predicate Abstraction}\label{sec:invariant-pred-abs}

\emph{Predicate abstraction}~\cite{graf1997predicate} computes invariants over a
finite Boolean lattice defined by a set of user-provided or automatically
discovered predicates.

\begin{definition}[Predicate Abstraction]\label{def:pred-abs}
Given a set of predicates $\{p_1, \ldots, p_n\}$, the abstract domain is the
Boolean lattice $2^{\{p_1, \ldots, p_n\}}$ ordered by implication.  The abstract
state at each program point is a Boolean combination of the predicates.
\end{definition}

The \textsc{BLAST}~\cite{henzinger2002blast} and \textsc{SLAM}~\cite{ball2001slam}
tools use predicate abstraction with CEGAR (Counterexample-Guided Abstraction
Refinement) to verify C programs.

\subsection{IC3/PDR}\label{sec:invariant-ic3}

IC3 (Incremental Construction by Inductive Checking), also known as PDR
(Property Directed Reachability)~\cite{bradley2011sat}, is a state-of-the-art
algorithm for invariant generation in the setting of hardware verification.
It works by iteratively constructing an inductive invariant that implies the
safety property.

The key idea is to maintain a sequence of frames $F_0, F_1, F_2, \ldots$ where
$F_i$ over-approximates the set of states reachable in at most $i$ steps.  The
algorithm attempts to \emph{block} bad states by adding clauses to the frames.

\subsection{Houdini}\label{sec:invariant-houdini}

\textsc{Houdini}~\cite{flanagan2001houdini} is an algorithm for invariant
discovery that works with a set of \emph{candidate invariants}---guesses
provided by the user or generated by heuristics.  It iteratively removes
candidates that are inconsistent with the program semantics until a fixed point
is reached.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Houdini Algorithm}\label{alg:houdini}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, candidate invariants $C = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$
\Ensure Subset $C' \subseteq C$ that is inductive
\State $C' \gets C$
\Repeat
  \State $C'_{\mathit{old}} \gets C'$
  \For{each $c_i \in C'$}
    \State Check if $\{C' \setminus \{c_i\} \land c_i\}\; \mathit{body}\; \{c_i\}$
    \If{not valid}
      \State $C' \gets C' \setminus \{c_i\}$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\Until{$C' = C'_{\mathit{old}}$}
\State \Return $C'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

