\section{Separation Logic for Heap Programs}\label{sec:deductive-separation}

\subsection{The Frame Problem}\label{sec:sep-frame}

Standard Hoare logic struggles with programs that manipulate the heap.  The
fundamental difficulty is the \emph{frame problem}: when a command modifies part
of the heap, the Hoare triple must explicitly state which parts of the heap are
\emph{not} modified.  For a program with $n$ heap-allocated data structures,
this requires reasoning about $n$ disjoint regions of memory.

\subsection{Separation Logic Assertions}\label{sec:sep-assertions}

Separation logic~\cite{reynolds2002separation,ohearn2019separation} extends
assertion logic with a \emph{separating conjunction} $*$ and a \emph{separating
implication} $\wand$.

\begin{definition}[Separation Logic Assertions]\label{def:sep-assert}
The assertions of separation logic are defined by:
\begin{align*}
  P, Q &::= \mathit{emp} \mid e \mapsto e' \mid P * Q \mid P \wand Q \mid P \land Q \mid P \lor Q \mid \exists x.\, P \mid \forall x.\, P
\end{align*}
The semantics is given with respect to a \emph{heap} $h$ (a partial function
from locations to values):
\begin{itemize}
\item $h \models \mathit{emp}$ iff $h$ is the empty heap.
\item $h \models e \mapsto e'$ iff $h = \{[\![e]\!] \mapsto [\![e']\!]\}$.
\item $h \models P * Q$ iff there exist disjoint heaps $h_1, h_2$ such that
      $h = h_1 \uplus h_2$, $h_1 \models P$, and $h_2 \models Q$.
\item $h \models P \wand Q$ iff for all heaps $h'$ disjoint from $h$,
      if $h' \models P$ then $h \uplus h' \models Q$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Separating Conjunction]\label{ex:sep-conj}
The assertion $x \mapsto 3 * y \mapsto 4$ asserts that $x$ and $y$ point to
\emph{distinct} memory cells containing 3 and 4 respectively.  This is stronger
than $x \mapsto 3 \land y \mapsto 4$, which allows $x$ and $y$ to alias.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Frame Rule}\label{sec:sep-frame-rule}

The signature achievement of separation logic is the \emph{frame rule}, which
allows local reasoning about heap modifications.

\begin{semrules}{Separation Logic}
  \frac{\{P\}\; c\; \{Q\}}{\{P * R\}\; c\; \{Q * R\}}
  \quad \textsc{Frame} \quad (\text{provided } \mathit{mod}(c) \cap \mathit{fv}(R) = \emptyset)
\end{semrules}

The frame rule states that if $c$ satisfies a specification $\{P\}\; c\; \{Q\}$,
then $c$ also satisfies $\{P * R\}\; c\; \{Q * R\}$ for any $R$ that describes
memory not modified by $c$.  This is the key to \emph{local reasoning}: we can
verify a program component by considering only the memory it accesses, without
reasoning about the rest of the heap.

\subsection{Linked List Verification}\label{sec:sep-list}

Separation logic enables elegant verification of linked data structures using
\emph{recursive predicates}.  The standard list segment predicate is:
\begin{align*}
  \mathit{ls}(x, y) &\triangleq (x = y \land \mathit{emp}) \lor (x \neq y \land \exists z.\, x \mapsto z * \mathit{ls}(z, y))
\end{align*}
This predicate describes a singly-linked list from $x$ to $y$, with each
separating conjunction asserting that the list nodes are disjoint.

\begin{insight}
Separation logic's frame rule is the formal counterpart of a principle that
experienced programmers apply intuitively: when reasoning about a function, you
only need to consider the memory it touches, not the entire heap.  This
\emph{locality} principle is what makes scalable verification of heap-manipulating
programs possible.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Tools Based on Separation Logic}\label{sec:sep-tools}

Several verification tools are built on separation logic:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textsc{Smallfoot}~\cite{calcagno2011smallfoot}: Automatic verification
      of heap-manipulating programs using a decidable fragment of separation
      logic.
\item \textsc{VeriFast}~\cite{jacobs2011verifast}: Verification of C and Java
      programs using separation logic with user-provided invariants.
\item \textsc{Iris}~\cite{jung2015iris}: A framework for higher-order
      concurrent separation logic, used to verify operating system kernels.
\end{itemize}

