\section{Modular Verification}\label{sec:deductive-modular}

\subsection{The Modular Approach}\label{sec:modular-approach}

\emph{Modular verification} verifies each program component (function, class,
module) independently, using \emph{contracts} (pre/post-condition pairs) to
specify the interface between components.

\begin{definition}[Module Contract]\label{def:contract}
A \emph{contract} for a function $f$ is a pair $(\mathit{Pre}_f, \mathit{Post}_f)$
such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item The caller must ensure $\mathit{Pre}_f$ before calling $f$.
\item The callee (implementation of $f$) must ensure $\mathit{Post}_f$ on return.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Modular Reasoning with Contracts}\label{sec:modular-reasoning}

When verifying a function $g$ that calls $f$, the verifier uses $f$'s contract
rather than $f$'s implementation:
\[
\frac{\{P\}\; \mathit{call}\ f\; \{Q\} \quad \text{where } P \implies \mathit{Pre}_f \text{ and } \mathit{Post}_f \implies Q}{\{P\}\; \mathit{call}\ f\; \{Q\}}
\]

This enables \emph{compositional} verification: the cost of verifying a program
grows linearly with its size (assuming contracts are provided), rather than
exponentially.

\subsection{Specification Inheritance}\label{sec:spec-inheritance}

In object-oriented programs, contracts can be inherited and refined through
subtyping.  The \emph{behavioral subtyping} principle requires that a subtype's
methods satisfy the supertype's contracts:
\[
\mathit{Pre}_{\mathit{sub}} \implies \mathit{Pre}_{\mathit{super}} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathit{Post}_{\mathit{super}} \implies \mathit{Post}_{\mathit{sub}}
\]
That is, the subtype must accept at least as many inputs (weaker precondition)
and guarantee at least as much on output (stronger postcondition).

