\section{Modern Verification Tools}\label{sec:deductive-tools}

\subsection{Dafny}\label{sec:deductive-dafny}

Dafny~\cite{leino2010dafny} is a verification-aware programming language
developed at Microsoft Research.  It combines an imperative programming language
with a specification language based on pre/post-conditions, loop invariants,
and ghost code.  Dafny uses the Boogie intermediate verification language and
the Z3 SMT solver to discharge verification conditions.

Key features of Dafny include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Pre/post-conditions:} \texttt{requires} and \texttt{ensures} clauses.
\item \textbf{Loop invariants:} \texttt{invariant} clauses on while loops.
\item \textbf{Ghost code:} Code that exists only for verification purposes.
\item \textbf{Decreases clauses:} For proving termination.
\item \textbf{Datatypes and collections:} Built-in support for reasoning about
      sequences, sets, and maps.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{F*}\label{sec:deductive-fstar}

F*~\cite{swamy2016fstar} is a general-purpose proof-oriented programming language
that combines dependent types, effect types, and an SMT-based automation layer.
F* can express very precise specifications (e.g., ``this function returns a
sorted permutation of its input'') and has been used to verify cryptographic
protocols, web browsers, and operating system kernels.

\subsection{Why3}\label{sec:deductive-why3}

Why3~\cite{filliatre2013why3} is a platform for deductive program verification.
It provides a rich specification language and can generate verification
conditions for multiple back-end provers (Coq, Isabelle, Z3, CVC4, Alt-Ergo).
Why3's \emph{diversification} strategy---sending the same VC to multiple
provers---increases the likelihood of successful automated proof.

\subsection{Vercors}\label{sec:deductive-vercors}

Vercors~\cite{vercors2020} is a verification framework for Java programs.  It
supports a rich specification language (including separation logic-style
permissions) and uses a multi-prover back-end.  Vercors is notable for its
support of concurrent program verification using permission-based separation
logic.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Comparison of Deductive Verification Tools}\label{tab:deductive-tools}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
\textbf{Tool} & \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Logic} & \textbf{Automation} & \textbf{Concurrency} \\
\hline
Dafny & Dafny & First-order + types & High (Z3) & Limited \\
F* & F* & Dependent types & Medium (Z3) & Via effects \\
Why3 & WhyML & First-order & High (multi-prover) & No \\
Vercors & Java & Separation logic & Medium (multi-prover) & Yes \\
VeriFast & C, Java & Separation logic & Medium & Yes \\
Iris & Coq & Higher-order sep. logic & Low (interactive) & Yes \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{historical}
The history of deductive verification begins with Hoare's 1969
paper~\cite{hoare1969axiomatic} introducing axiomatic semantics.  The first
verification systems---including the Stanford Pascal
Verifier~\cite{cheatham1979stanford} and the Affirm system---date to the late
1970s.  The field stagnated for decades due to the high cost of user interaction
with theorem provers.  The development of powerful SMT solvers in the 2000s,
particularly Z3~\cite{demoura2008z3}, transformed the field by automating the
discharge of verification conditions.  Modern tools like Dafny and F* represent
the current state of the art, making deductive verification practical for
non-trivial programs.
\end{historical}

