\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:deductive-summary}

Deductive verification provides the strongest possible guarantees about program
correctness: mathematical proof that a program satisfies its specification for
all inputs.  The key challenges remain the need for user-provided annotations
(invariants, contracts) and the difficulty of scaling to large programs.

Active research areas include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Automated invariant discovery:} Reducing the annotation burden.
\item \textbf{Concurrent verification:} Scaling separation logic to concurrent programs.
\item \textbf{Integration with development workflows:} Making verification
      practical in everyday software engineering.
\item \textbf{Proof-carrying code:} Attaching proofs to compiled code for
      secure mobile code (see \cref{ch:proof-carrying}).
\end{itemize}

The textbook by Huth and Ryan~\cite{huth2004logic} provides an accessible
introduction, while the monograph by Havelund et al.~\cite{havelund2020verified}
covers modern tools and techniques.
